00:00:02.030 --> 00:00:04.160 align:start position:0%
the creatures of the deep are hard to  fathom that's because collecting them  takes equipment like nets and suction  tubes which can injure the delicate  animals now scientists have come up with  a kinder gentler way of handling these  creatures inspired by the mechanisms  that move kids toys and even our own  hands  researchers from the VESA Institute at  Harvard University invented a prototype  gentle mechanical hand that can safely  capture deep-sea creatures the device is  composed of two-dimensional shapes that  fold to create a three-dimensional  enclosure think of it like origami where  a single piece of paper can be shaped  into a crane or a ball only in this case  the shapes and folds are predicted  mathematically the prototype had five  main panels that are comprised of two  smaller panels all the panels are  connected to a central link that's  controlled by a rotary actuator this  coordinates the panels so they can be  folded together at the same time by  closing fingers into a fist and similar  to our hands this device is also  attached to an arm which can be mounted  on an underwater vehicle and controlled  remotely the grabber can dive deep while  scientists watch safely from their ship  not only is it safe for the researchers  whether it's safe for sea creatures as  well that's because of the soft silicon  edges of the panels the scientists can  stop the cage from closing at any time  as they can watch its progress by video  future versions of the device might  include sensors that can measure in  organisms vitals or a 3d scanner that  can reveal how their bodies act  differently at these depths compared to  the low pressure surface given its  simple design the mechanical hand might  one day be kitted up for work in outer  space perhaps is a way of manipulating  solar panels to power satellites but for  now  this device is already lending a hand to  deep-sea research  [Music]  [Music]
 

